Subject: Tweak: override retail menus, the pause menu, Options and ONLINE
Upstream-base: 8b9d92dd88b4c727cc6359ee4cd5ba128cced7b8

A development switch ("Override retail menus", tweak bit 15): Recharged's own
versions of the game's menus stand in for the original ones as they are
built: the match's pause menu (art/fe/pausemenu_v3.fen; the Strikers 101
lessons have their own and keep it), the front end's Options, the main
menu's OPTIONS (art/fe/options_main_menu.fen), and the main menu's ONLINE.

The game's scenes still run and keep their flow: the pause menu pauses and
resumes the match with its players' pointers and buttons, plays its slides
and its hover and selection sounds, opens its pages (Choose Sides,
Statistics or the challenge's Objectives, Controller Map) and asks before
quitting; the Options keep their camera move, slides and way back. Only their
drawing is left out (FERender::RenderScene): the host reads the scenes, their
buttons and the players' cursors after each frame (src/platform/
menu_snapshot.cpp) and draws Recharged's menus with them over the picture
(src/runtime/host_menus.cpp, the UI kit in src/ui).

The pause menu's Audio Options and Visual Options open Recharged's Sound and
Graphics pages over the menu instead of the game's pages (PauseMenuScene::
OnOptionPointerPress asks mscharged_tweak_menu_press_taken; the menu then
stays as it is, ready again when the page closes). Those pages hold the game's
own settings too (its volume bars, the camera's zoom), which the host sets
through commands done once a frame in BeginFrameTask
(mscharged_tweak_menus_frame), as is resuming from a page.

The main menu's ONLINE (SHMainMenu::SelectItem) opens Recharged's online menu
over the wheel instead of the game's way (its checks, the save's load, the
Mii selection, the login and its online menu); its WII ONLINE presses ONLINE
again through the same function, let through this time, so the game's own
online is unchanged.

The players' cursors (wiicursor.fen's cursor0-3) are the game's own on top of
those menus: while one shows, FERender::RenderTimeLineAsset marks a cursor's
drawing, and its pictures (RenderImageInstance, the instance's quad and
matrix) and its number (nlFont::DrawString, the glyph quads and their matrix)
are handed to the host instead of being drawn under the menu. The host draws
them with the game's own textures, at the game's positions and colours, with
the cursor's own slides (waiting, the A over a button, its pulsing ring).

Off, nothing changes. The guarded hooks exist only in the opt-in native game
module.

